```{r setup, include = FALSE}
stopifnot(require(knitr))
# Nothing here is evaluated. Every chunk is code the reader runs on their own
# machine, and evaluating any of it records the machine that built the vignette:
# before #342 seven chunks were evaluated and the rendered file named one
# contributor's home directory 21 times and carried 77 lines of compiler
# diagnostics from it. Setting eval once for the document rather than chunk by
# chunk means a chunk added later inherits it.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  eval = FALSE
)
```

[e1]: https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html

`bayesnec` fits its models with `brms` [@Burkner2017; @Burkner2018], which
passes them to Stan (<https://mc-stan.org/>). Stan does not interpret a model.
Each model is translated into C++, compiled into a program, and that program is
run. A C++ toolchain is therefore required to fit anything at all, and this is
also why the first fit of a session takes longer than the ones after it.

`brms` samples through either of two back ends. `rstan` [@stan2020] is installed
as a dependency of `brms` whether or not it is used as the sampler, so it is
present on any machine that can load `brms`. `cmdstanr` is on neither CRAN nor
the dependency list of `bayesnec`, and is what this package's own vignettes are
built with; `options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")` is the line that selects it.
The instructions below install `cmdstanr`, because it is the back end the
package is developed and tested against. The `rstan` route is documented by the
Stan project at <https://github.com/stan-dev/rstan/wiki/RStan-Getting-Started>.

# Software requirements

| Software | Purpose |
|---|---|
| R | the language the rest of it runs in |
| A C++ toolchain | compiles each model before it is fitted |
| CmdStan | the sampler that runs the compiled model |

The toolchain is the part that differs by operating system, and the part that
fails most often. On Windows it is Rtools, on macOS the Xcode Command Line
Tools, and on Linux the standard build tools.

## Route by operating system

Windows is described in full below. The difficulties Stan had on Windows
historically came from `rstan`, which requires matched versions of several
compiled packages; `cmdstanr` avoids most of that.

Linux is described next, and was verified on 2026-09-11 under Debian with R
4.6.1 and CmdStan 2.39.0. macOS is not covered in the same detail, because these
steps have not been verified on it; that section points at the upstream
instructions instead. The Windows Subsystem for Linux is a fallback for one
situation only and is described last. Do not start there.

What determines whether an installation works is the check in the *Verification*
section, not which of these routes was followed to reach it.

# Windows

## R and Rtools

Install R first, from <https://cran.r-project.org/bin/windows/base/>, and then
the Rtools that matches it, from
<https://cran.r-project.org/bin/windows/Rtools/>. Accept the default
installation location.

Rtools is versioned against the minor version of R: Rtools45 is for R 4.5.x and
Rtools44 for R 4.4.x. CRAN's Rtools page states the current pairing. A mismatch
is the most common single cause of a failed setup, and the error it produces
names no version number, so install R first and read that page rather than
reusing an Rtools already on the machine.

## R packages

`cmdstanr` is not on CRAN, so it comes from the Stan project's own repository:

```{r install_cmdstanr}
install.packages("cmdstanr",
                 repos = c("https://stan-dev.r-universe.dev",
                           getOption("repos")))
```

`bayesnec` and the rest come from CRAN, and installing `bayesnec` installs
`brms` with it:

```{r install_bayesnec}
install.packages("bayesnec")
```

## The CmdStan installation

Confirm the compiler is configured first. On a current Rtools this passes
without doing anything; `fix = TRUE` repairs the problems it can rather than
only reporting them.

```{r toolchain_fix}
cmdstanr::check_cmdstan_toolchain(fix = TRUE)
```

Then install CmdStan. Three things are set here, and each of them has been
observed to stop a fresh installation on Windows.

```{r install_cmdstan}
dir.create("C:/cmdstan")           # install_cmdstan() does not create it
options(timeout = 1800)            # R's default of 60 seconds is too short
cmdstanr::install_cmdstan(dir = "C:/cmdstan")
```

The directory has to exist first. `install_cmdstan()` does not create it and
stops with `Assertion on 'dir' failed: Directory 'C:/cmdstan' does not exist.`

The download needs a longer timeout than R's default. The CmdStan archive is
about 49 MB, and the `timeout` argument of `install_cmdstan()` does not govern
the download while `options(timeout)` does. Measured on a connection delivering
about 500 KB/s, the download failed with `Download of CmdStan failed with error`
at the default and completed in 4.9 minutes at `timeout = 1800`, with nothing
else changed. Set it in any case: it has no effect where the download would
have completed anyway.

The installation path has to be a plain one. The default location is inside the
home directory, and on many Windows machines, particularly ones managed by an
employer, that directory is synchronised by OneDrive, where the CmdStan build
fails. The same failure occurs where the path contains a space or a character
outside the standard English alphabet, which happens where a Windows user name
does. `C:/cmdstan` avoids all three. Installing there rather than in the default
location changes nothing else about how CmdStan is used, beyond the `.Rprofile`
line below.

Because `C:/cmdstan` is not the default location, R has to be told where to find
it in every later session. Run `usethis::edit_r_profile()`, or open `.Rprofile`
in your home directory, and add the line below with the version number that was
installed. Without it, every new session reports CmdStan as not installed.

```{r rprofile}
options(cmdstanr_cmdstan_path = "C:/cmdstan/cmdstan-2.39.0")
```

That file is also where the back end belongs, so that it does not have to be set
again in each session.

```{r backend}
options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")
```

## Recurring problems on Windows

### A partly built CmdStan directory

Where `install_cmdstan()` fails, run it again with these arguments.

```{r reinstall}
cmdstanr::install_cmdstan(overwrite = TRUE, quiet = FALSE, dir = "C:/cmdstan")
```

`overwrite = TRUE` is the part that matters. An attempt that fails partway
through the build leaves a partly built directory behind, and a second attempt
without `overwrite` declines to replace it, which produces an error resembling
the first one. `quiet = FALSE` only prints more of the build output, which is
worth having because the default output is truncated and does not identify the
stage that failed.

### Chains that do not start after an Rtools upgrade

A model compiles and `cmdstanr` then reports `No chains finished successfully`,
which names neither a cause nor a file. The usual reason is that Rtools changed
after CmdStan was installed. CmdStan builds its own `tbb.dll`, linked against
the toolchain that built it, so a CmdStan built under Rtools44 requires
libraries that Rtools45 does not provide: the model still compiles, and every
chain then exits immediately with `STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND`. Rebuilding CmdStan
resolves it.

```{r rebuild}
cmdstanr::install_cmdstan(overwrite = TRUE, dir = "C:/cmdstan")
```

Anyone who upgrades R and Rtools after installing CmdStan will meet this.

### The `cmdstanr` version and the Rtools it expects

Rtools is installed and `cmdstanr` reports it as missing. `cmdstanr` chooses
which Rtools to look for from the R version, and the mapping changed between
releases: 0.9.0 sends R 4.5.x to Rtools45, while 0.8.0 sent every R from 4.4
onward to Rtools44. An older `cmdstanr` alongside Rtools45 therefore searches for
a toolchain that was never installed. Install `cmdstanr` 0.9.0 or later, which
is what the r-universe repository above supplies.

### Slow compilation

Windows Defender inspects each newly compiled program, and a compile that should
take under a minute can take several. Excluding the CmdStan directory from
real-time scanning resolves it, where the machine's policy allows.

### Compilation blocked by policy

Some managed machines prevent a newly compiled program from running at all.
This is a policy decision rather than a configuration problem, and it cannot be
worked around from within R. The *Windows Subsystem for Linux* section describes
the alternative.

# macOS

These steps have not been verified on macOS. They point at the upstream
instructions, which the projects themselves maintain, and the check in the
*Verification* section is what settles whether the installation works.

1. Install R from <https://cran.r-project.org/bin/macosx/>, choosing the build
   for your processor: the Apple Silicon and Intel builds differ.
2. Install the Xcode Command Line Tools by running `xcode-select --install` in
   Terminal. Some packages also require `gfortran`, and CRAN's macOS tools page
   states the current requirement.
3. Install the R packages as in the Windows section above.
4. Follow the CmdStanR getting-started vignette
   (<https://mc-stan.org/cmdstanr/articles/cmdstanr.html>) for
   `check_cmdstan_toolchain()` and `install_cmdstan()`. The default installation
   path causes no difficulty on macOS, so `dir` need not be set.

# Linux

Install R through the distribution's package manager or from CRAN, together with
the standard build tools: `build-essential` on Debian and Ubuntu, or the
equivalent development group on other distributions. Install the R packages as
in the Windows section, then run `cmdstanr::check_cmdstan_toolchain()` followed
by `cmdstanr::install_cmdstan()`. The default path causes no difficulty here
either.

# The Windows Subsystem for Linux

WSL runs a Linux system inside Windows. Use it only where compilation on Windows
is blocked by security policy on a machine you cannot change. It is not a
simpler route and is not recommended otherwise.

It requires a second installation of R inside Linux, a second package library to
keep track of, a different way of referring to your files, and further editor
configuration to work with the Linux session. Someone not already comfortable at
a command line takes on a second system to learn alongside the modelling.

# Verification

Two checks. The first inspects the toolchain and the second uses it, so run
them in that order.

```{r verify_toolchain}
cmdstanr::check_cmdstan_toolchain()
cmdstanr::cmdstan_version()
```

A configured toolchain reports

```
The C++ toolchain required for CmdStan is setup properly!
```

and `cmdstan_version()` returns the version string of the installation R is
using. An error from either is a toolchain or path problem, and nothing below
will work until it is resolved.

The second check fits a model, and it is the one that matters, because it
exercises the whole path: `bnec()` calls `brms`, `brms` writes a Stan program,
CmdStan compiles it, and the compiled program samples.

```{r verify_fit}
library(bayesnec)
data(nec_data)

options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")
set.seed(333)
fit <- bnec(y ~ crf(x, model = "nec4param"), data = nec_data,
            family = Beta(link = "identity"), chains = 2, seed = 333)
summary(fit)
```

A successful run compiles one Stan program, prints the sampling progress of two
chains, and returns an object of class `bayesnecfit`. Its `summary()` reports the
four parameters of the **nec4param** equation --- **bot**, **top**, **beta** and
**nec** --- each with an `Rhat` near 1.00, then the *NEC* estimate with its
credible interval, and a Bayesian R-squared. Two chains are enough to show that
the toolchain works. The other vignettes take the `brms` default of four, which
is what an analysis should use.

With that fit returning, the modelling vignettes can be followed;
[Single model usage][e1] is the place to start.

